    </div>
    </li> <li> <para-num num="[0383]"> </para-num> <div id="p-0455" num="0383" class="description-line">The blends were compression molded into films of 15 mils thick using a Carver hot press. These films were then sandwiched between Teflon sheets, and heated at 190° C. at 0.4 MPa for 9 min. Afterwards, the blends were cooled by putting them into ambient temperature water.</div>
    </li> <li> <para-num num="[0384]"> </para-num> <div id="p-0456" num="0384" class="description-line">The phase morphology of the blends was studied using tapping mode Atomic Force Microscopy (AFM). Compression molded samples were first polished using an ultramicrotome (Reichert-Jung Ultracut E) at −100° C. perpendicular to the plaques near the center of the core area. A thin section was placed on a mica surface for AFM imaging using a DI NanoScope IV, MultiMode AFM operating in Tapping Mode with phase detection. The tip was tuned to a voltage of 3V and the tapping ratio was 0.76-0.83. Nano-sensor tips were used with tip parameters of: L=235 μm, tip ratio=5-10 nm, Spring constant=37-55 N/m, F<sub>0</sub>=159-164 kHz.</div>
    </li> <li> <para-num num="[0385]"> </para-num> <div id="p-0457" num="0385" class="description-line">In the sample from <figure-callout id="4" label="Mixture" filenames="US20080269366A1-20081030-D00002.png,US20080269366A1-20081030-D00013.png" state="{{state}}">Mixture</figure-callout> 4, shown in <figref idrefs="DRAWINGS">FIG. 13</figref>, HDPE inclusions within a PP matrix were observed. The domain size of HDPE predominately ranged from 1 to 10 μm with sharp interfaces. In <figure-callout id="5" label="Mixture" filenames="US20080269366A1-20081030-D00005.png,US20080269366A1-20081030-D00006.png" state="{{state}}">Mixture</figure-callout> 5, shown in <figref idrefs="DRAWINGS">FIG. 14</figref>, and <figure-callout id="6" label="Mixture" filenames="US20080269366A1-20081030-D00021.png" state="{{state}}">Mixture</figure-callout> 6, shown in <figref idrefs="DRAWINGS">FIG. 15</figref>, the addition of Comparative B<sup>1 </sup>or Polymer Example 21 respectively, resulted in a reduction of the HDPE domain size to &lt;5 μm. The particles had a shell core morphology with Comparative B<sup>1 </sup>or Polymer Example 21 forming the shell and HDPE as the core. Being lower modulus, Comparative B<sup>1 </sup>or Polymer Example 21 appeared as dark areas in the images. Some Comparative B<sup>1 </sup>or Polymer Example 21 individual particles were also present.</div>
    </li> <li> <para-num num="[0386]"> </para-num> <div id="p-0458" num="0386" class="description-line">Uniaxial tensile behavior was measured at ambient temperature using microtensile specimens according to ASTM D 1708. Samples were stretched with an Instron 5564 at a rate of 500% min<sup>−1 </sup>and a temperature of 21° C. Tensile strength and elongation at break are reported from an average of 5 specimens.</div>
    </li> <li> <para-num num="[0387]"> </para-num> <div id="p-0459" num="0387" class="description-line">The tensile curves of samples from <figure-callout id="4" label="Mixtures" filenames="US20080269366A1-20081030-D00002.png,US20080269366A1-20081030-D00013.png" state="{{state}}"> <figure-callout id="5" label="Mixtures" filenames="US20080269366A1-20081030-D00005.png,US20080269366A1-20081030-D00006.png" state="{{state}}"> <figure-callout id="6" label="Mixtures" filenames="US20080269366A1-20081030-D00021.png" state="{{state}}">Mixtures</figure-callout> </figure-callout> </figure-callout> 4, 5 and 6 are shown in <figref idrefs="DRAWINGS">FIG. 16</figref>. The samples from <figure-callout id="4" label="Mixture" filenames="US20080269366A1-20081030-D00002.png,US20080269366A1-20081030-D00013.png" state="{{state}}">Mixture</figure-callout> 4 showed poor mechanical properties with low elongation at break (&lt;200%) and low tensile strength (18 MPa). In the samples from <figure-callout id="5" label="Mixture" filenames="US20080269366A1-20081030-D00005.png,US20080269366A1-20081030-D00006.png" state="{{state}}">Mixture</figure-callout> 5, containing Comparative B<sup>1</sup>, improvements in ultimate properties are not observed despite the change in morphology. Yield stress is actually decreased. In the samples from <figure-callout id="6" label="Mixture" filenames="US20080269366A1-20081030-D00021.png" state="{{state}}">Mixture</figure-callout> 6, containing Polymer Example 21, substantial improvement in tensile properties was observed over samples from <figure-callout id="4" label="Mixture" filenames="US20080269366A1-20081030-D00002.png,US20080269366A1-20081030-D00013.png" state="{{state}}">Mixture</figure-callout> 4 and <figure-callout id="5" label="Mixture" filenames="US20080269366A1-20081030-D00005.png,US20080269366A1-20081030-D00006.png" state="{{state}}">Mixture</figure-callout> 5. The samples from <figure-callout id="6" label="Mixture" filenames="US20080269366A1-20081030-D00021.png" state="{{state}}">Mixture</figure-callout> 6 had elongation at break of about 860% with about 41 MPa tensile strength. A summary of the ultimate properties is listed in Table 12.</div>
    </li> <li> <div id="p-0460" num="0000" class="description-line">
      <tables id="TABLE-US-00016" num="00016">
